  <p class="kicker">Part IV · Can it run without you? · Chapter 22</p>
  <h1>Measuring the Right Three Things</h1>
  <p class="lede">Thirty seconds of numbers, every morning — that was
  <a href="21-meetings-that-arent-theater.html">chapter twenty-one</a>'s instruction, and it leaves
  open which numbers, and who reads them. Not the total. The total is the one figure that tells a person who
  is not you nothing they can act on. What follows is three numbers underneath it, and the reason
  each one stops telling the truth the day you start managing to it.</p>

  <div class="decision">
    <span class="tag">The decision</span>
    Which three weekly figures the business reads at the morning meeting without you in the room — chosen so that a
    capable person who did not build the business can tell from them whether it is moving, what it
    is losing, and how much of it rests on how few customers — and which figures you deliberately
    leave for a monthly look, so that the weekly three stay readable.
  </div>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 1</span> The situation</h2>

    <p>You know the number. Last month's takings, this month's so far, the week's cash figure from
    <a href="12-reading-your-own-business.html">chapter twelve</a> — you can say them without
    looking, and when someone asks how the business is doing, one of them is the answer. The
    trouble is that they are answers only to you. You hear a month's total and know, from
    everything else in your head, that it is fine or that it is not; the person you hired hears the
    same figure and knows only that it is a number.</p>

    <p>Now that other people are in the business, the figures have to work without the head that
    used to interpret them. That is a different requirement from knowing your own numbers. A figure
    that needs you to explain it is one more thing that only you can do, and
    <a href="17-you-are-the-bottleneck.html">chapter seventeen</a> already ranked those. So the
    question is not which numbers matter — dozens do — but <strong>which few can be read, weekly,
    by someone who is not you, and read correctly</strong>. And behind that, quieter: what happens
    to a number once the people reading it are also the people being judged by it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 2</span> What actually happened</h2>

    <p>ConvertKit was in this book at <a href="08-asking-for-money.html">chapter eight</a>, at
    $1,207 a month, its bottom. In January 2019, past a million a month in recurring revenue and
    with its dashboard open to the public, Nathan Barry wrote a walkthrough of what he actually
    looks at, because the attention that came with the milestone had brought readers who took the
    metrics at surface level. What he wrote is a list of the questions he asks of the figures, in
    order, with the figures attached.</p>

    <p>He starts where everyone does, and immediately moves off it. Monthly recurring revenue is,
    for him, the most important metric in the business, but its growth is a summary of five
    movements — new, expansion, reactivation, contraction and churn — so the graph he reads is not
    the total but the breakout, where the monthly change is <em>"all over the map. From as high as
    $73,000 net new in March to a couple negative months in the summer."</em></p>

    <p>Then loss, which is not one number. Revenue churn is the share of the revenue base lost in a
    month; user churn is the share of customers lost. They diverge for a reason he can name:
    ConvertKit's beginner customers leave more often than its larger ones, so user churn there is
    always the higher of the two. Then a third version, net revenue churn, which nets upgrades
    against the losses, and the figure: <em>"At ConvertKit we are at 2.2% net revenue churn."</em>
    Then the customer count, read the same way as revenue — not the total but the net new each
    month — and the admission that goes with it, that it is both interesting and depressing to
    watch so many new accounts arrive each month and be cancelled out by so many leaving.</p>

    <p>Then a ratio, an average, and a split. A quick ratio — new and expansion revenue over
    churned and contracted — at <em>"1.47, which is fairly low"</em>, though the highest of the
    three open companies he compares it with, <em>"Buffer at 1.08 and Hubstaff at 1.38"</em>.
    Average revenue per customer: <em>"Buffer is at $21 per customer and ConvertKit is at $57 per
    customer."</em> And last, concentration: <em>"we have 20,812 generating $1,188,580 in MRR"</em>,
    of whom everyone paying more than the base plan is <em>"6,994 customers (33% of the total)
    generating $811,117 in MRR (68% of the total)"</em> — or, as he puts it the other way round,
    two thirds of the customers producing a third of the revenue.</p>

    <p>That is seven, and Barry nowhere says fewer would do. The compression that
    follows is this book's, made on the reader's behalf and for a reason the post supplies: a
    founder auditing his own dashboard can hold seven; a business that has to run without him
    cannot. What the post does show is an order. Movement first, loss second, and the rest
    introduced as refinements of those two — which is the order the next section keeps.</p>

    <p>One more thing in the post is not a metric. Once in it, one event moves three numbers three
    different ways. Each November ConvertKit pushes annual plans, sold at a discount: net revenue
    that month climbs <em>"as high as $3 million"</em>, while MRR <em>drops</em>, because the
    discount is a contraction — and revenue churn spikes with it, then falls again once the
    annual-plan contraction has moved out of the thirty-day window the figure is computed over.
    Same business, same customers, one decision, three figures swinging on a sales calendar. And on
    comparing churn between companies, he recalls reading an S-1 from a company with net negative
    churn and realising that it did not count any account cancelling in its first ninety days as
    churn at all — convenient, he says, and no wonder. A number nobody had to falsify. They only
    had to define it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="move">
    <h2><span class="num">Move 3</span> The reasoning</h2>

    <h3>Movement, loss, concentration</h3>

    <p><strong>Three figures, read weekly by someone other than you: what came in and what went out
    underneath the total; what you lost, counted as customers and as money separately; and how much
    of the money rests on how few of the customers.</strong> The ground for choosing these three is what each lets a person who is not you see.</p>

    <p>For a business with no subscriptions — a bakery, a two-partner practice — the three are the same figures under plainer names, this book's translation and not Barry's: what came in against what went out, by week; the regulars who stopped coming, counted as people and as money; and how much of the month rests on the three largest accounts.</p>

    <p><em>Movement</em> is the total taken apart. The total says the month was up; the breakout
    says whether it was up because new customers came or because old ones spent more, and whether
    the quiet loss underneath was small or merely covered. Barry's summer had negative months
    inside a year that rose. For a business that does not sell subscriptions the breakout is
    coarser and still exists: new customers this week against returning ones, from the record <a href="10-the-first-ten-customers.html">chapter ten</a> started and
    <a href="11-what-happens-after-the-sale.html">chapter eleven</a> added the renew-and-reorder
    column to. <em>Loss</em>
    is counted twice because the two counts disagree in an instructive way. Losing five customers
    is one thing if they were the five smallest and another if one of them was the account that
    paid for the shop's rent; user churn and revenue churn are the two answers, and which is higher
    tells you who is leaving. <em>Concentration</em> is the figure you can state and least like to: the share of the money
    that comes from the top handful. Barry's is a third of the customers for two thirds of the
    revenue, and he gives the split without a verdict. The verdict he does give is on the average
    — he wants it to rise, <em>"but not see it so high that we are beholden to a few
    customers"</em> — and that is the same worry from the other end: the average can rise because
    everyone pays more, or because a few pay for everyone.
    <a href="14-what-a-customer-is-worth.html">Chapter fourteen</a>'s segments are the same figure
    from the other side.</p>

    <p>What is left out, and why. The quick ratio and average revenue per customer are arithmetic on
    the first two figures; the cash figure is not left out at all: it is the one
    <a href="12-reading-your-own-business.html">chapter twelve</a> already has you reading weekly,
    it measures survival rather than the business, and it stays where chapter twenty-one put it.
    Chapter twelve's other two — committed demand and the quantity that leads revenue — are the
    movement figure before it lands; if you have kept them, the first of this chapter's three is
    already half filled in. Net revenue against recurring is a distinction a subscription business
    needs and most of this book's readers do not. Those go in the monthly look, and they stay out
    of the thirty seconds because a person reading seven numbers aloud is
    reading none of them.</p>

    <h3>The moment a measure becomes a target</h3>

    <p>The reason to keep the three separate, and to have someone other than you read them, is the
    quiet thing in the post. Every figure on that dashboard is honest, and one November decision moved three of them in three
    directions at once — net revenue up, MRR down, churn up and then down again on a thirty-day
    window — while the customers themselves were the same people, now paying yearly. Barry
    knows this, which is why he can read his own numbers. <strong>A person who does not know it
    will manage to the figure, and a figure that is managed to stops measuring what it was chosen
    to measure.</strong> The S-1 company did not lie; it had a definition of churn under which a customer leaving in the
    first ninety days was not a loss, and Barry's word for that is "convenient". A target set
    against such a figure — his own for the year is net revenue churn <em>"down to 0%"</em>, on a
    dashboard whose definitions he does not set — is safe only for as long as the definition is not
    the goal-setter's to move. Yours will be.</p>

    <p>The defence is not to stop measuring. It is to fix the definition before anyone is judged by
    the figure, in writing, and to change it only in the open. A lost customer is lost on the day you wrote down in advance — thirty days without a return,
    sixty, a cancelled contract — and not on a later day chosen once the number is in; a discount
    that lowers the monthly figure is written down as a discount; the concentration figure is read
    as a share with the customers named behind it, not as a percentage alone.
    And the person reading the three aloud should be someone whose week does not improve when the
    number does. That is the limit of the advice: it keeps a figure honest against ordinary
    pressure, not against a business that wants to deceive itself, and it is worth nothing for the
    fourth thing you did not measure, which is where the surprise will come from.</p>

    <p>Three is also a claim about your size, and so is weekly. Barry looks at his breakout monthly —
    <em>"Monthly is as granular as you need to go"</em> — and a business of ConvertKit's shape has
    the staff to read seven and the churn to need them. At two or five people, on chapter twelve's
    rule, the cadence follows the operating cycle, and for most readers of this book that is the
    week; the three are what fits in a morning, and the test of whether they are the right three
    is not this chapter's argument but Monday's fifth item: whether, after a quarter of reading them,
    anything happened to the business that they did not show first.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="monday">
    <h2>Monday</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Write the three definitions on one page before you write any numbers.</strong> What counts as a new customer, what counts as a lost one and on which day, and which customers make up "the top". Date the page. The definitions do not change without a note on the page saying when and why.</li>
      <li><strong>Fill in last week's three from the records you already keep.</strong> New against returning; customers lost and money lost, as two numbers; the share of the week's money from the top customers as you defined them in item one, with their names. If any of the three cannot be filled from what you record now, the gap is item one for next week, not a reason to skip it.</li>
      <li><strong>Hand the reading to someone else.</strong> The thirty seconds in chapter twenty-one's meeting are theirs, not yours, and the person should be someone the numbers do not pay. You listen.</li>
      <li><strong>Put the rest on a monthly page.</strong> Averages, ratios, the cash figure's trend — reviewed once a month, by you, and not read aloud weekly. If you find yourself adding a number to the three and the cash figure, take one away.</li>
      <li><strong>After a quarter, write down what surprised you.</strong> Every event that mattered — the lost account, the slow month, the supplier — and whether one of the three moved before it happened. If none did, the three are wrong for your business, and this list starts again with different ones.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <div class="owed">
    <p class="case-head"><span class="who">What would prove this chapter wrong</span></p>

    <p>What is owed is a business that read three well-chosen figures, honestly defined, weekly, by
    someone who was not the owner — and was blindsided anyway by something the three could not
    have shown, or, worse, was steered wrong by them: a concentration figure that read as healthy
    while the top customers were all the same fragile kind, a loss figure that stayed flat while the
    quality of what came in fell. ConvertKit's post cannot supply that; it is one founder reading
    seven numbers he already understands, and it reports no decision made on them and no surprise
    they missed. The chapter's own compression to three is an argument, and the honest statement of
    its risk is that it is made for a reader the post was not written for, which is why the last
    Monday item exists: the quarter's surprises are the counter-case, kept by you.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="reading">
    <h2>Reading</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="src">Source ·</span> <a href="https://nathanbarry.com/metrics/">Nathan Barry, "Understanding ConvertKit's open metrics"</a>, 14 January 2019 — the five components of MRR movement and the March-to-summer swing, the user/revenue/net revenue churn distinctions and the 2.2% figure, the quick ratio and its two comparators, the ARPU comparison with Buffer, the customer and revenue totals and the concentration split, the November annual-plan effect on net revenue, MRR and revenue churn, and the S-1 churn-definition anecdote.</li>
      <li><span class="src">Note ·</span> A founder's own account, the weaker evidence class this book uses for every founder post, attributed as such. The figures are his transcription of ConvertKit's own Baremetrics dashboard, which the post says is public but which this book has not queried; the concentration split is a same-day search on one export, not a stable segmentation. The "three things" of the title are this book's selection, made in Move 2 and Move 3 as such; Barry describes seven and does not say fewer would serve.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

