    68	  <section class="move">
    69	    <h2><span class="num">Move 2</span> What actually happened</h2>
    70	
    71	    <p>ConvertKit was in this book at <a href="08-asking-for-money.html">chapter eight</a>, at
    72	    $1,207 a month, its bottom. In January 2019, past a million a month in recurring revenue and
    73	    with its dashboard open to the public, Nathan Barry wrote a walkthrough of what he actually
    74	    looks at, because the attention that came with the milestone had brought readers who took the
    75	    metrics at surface level. What he wrote is a list of the questions he asks of the figures, in
    76	    order, with the figures attached.</p>
    77	
    78	    <p>He starts where everyone does, and immediately moves off it. Monthly recurring revenue is,
    79	    for him, the most important metric in the business, but its growth is a summary of five
    80	    movements — new, expansion, reactivation, contraction and churn — so the graph he reads is not
    81	    the total but the breakout, where the monthly change is <em>"all over the map. From as high as
    82	    $73,000 net new in March to a couple negative months in the summer."</em></p>
    83	
    84	    <p>Then loss, which is not one number. Revenue churn is the share of the revenue base lost in a
    85	    month; user churn is the share of customers lost. They diverge for a reason he can name:
    86	    ConvertKit's beginner customers leave more often than its larger ones, so user churn there is
    87	    always the higher of the two. Then a third version, net revenue churn, which nets upgrades
    88	    against the losses, and the figure: <em>"At ConvertKit we are at 2.2% net revenue churn."</em>
    89	    Then the customer count, read the same way as revenue — not the total but the net new each
    90	    month — and the admission that goes with it, that it is both interesting and depressing to
    91	    watch so many new accounts arrive each month and be cancelled out by so many leaving.</p>
    92	
    93	    <p>Then a ratio, an average, and a split. A quick ratio — new and expansion revenue over
    94	    churned and contracted — at <em>"1.47, which is fairly low"</em>, though the highest of the
    95	    three open companies he compares it with, <em>"Buffer at 1.08 and Hubstaff at 1.38"</em>.
    96	    Average revenue per customer: <em>"Buffer is at $21 per customer and ConvertKit is at $57 per
    97	    customer."</em> And last, concentration: <em>"we have 20,812 generating $1,188,580 in MRR"</em>,
    98	    of whom everyone paying more than the base plan is <em>"6,994 customers (33% of the total)
    99	    generating $811,117 in MRR (68% of the total)"</em> — or, as he puts it the other way round,
   100	    two thirds of the customers producing a third of the revenue.</p>
   101	
   102	    <p>That is seven, and Barry nowhere says fewer would do. The compression that
   103	    follows is this book's, made on the reader's behalf and for a reason the post supplies: a
   104	    founder auditing his own dashboard can hold seven; a business that has to run without him
   105	    cannot. What the post does show is an order. Movement first, loss second, and the rest
   106	    introduced as refinements of those two — which is the order the next section keeps.</p>
   107	
   108	    <p>One more thing in the post is not a metric. Once in it, one event moves three numbers three
   109	    different ways. Each November ConvertKit pushes annual plans, sold at a discount: net revenue
   110	    that month climbs <em>"as high as $3 million"</em>, while MRR <em>drops</em>, because the
   111	    discount is a contraction — and revenue churn spikes with it, then falls again once the
   112	    annual-plan contraction has moved out of the thirty-day window the figure is computed over.
   113	    Same business, same customers, one decision, three figures swinging on a sales calendar. And on
   114	    comparing churn between companies, he recalls reading an S-1 from a company with net negative
   115	    churn and realising that it did not count any account cancelling in its first ninety days as
   116	    churn at all — convenient, he says, and no wonder. A number nobody had to falsify. They only
   117	    had to define it.</p>
   118	  </section>
